WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.660
Environments change.

00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:02.365
Threats evolve.

00:00:02.365 --> 00:00:03.790
Business processes adapt.

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:05.625
Detection rules should evolve as well.

00:00:05.625 --> 00:00:08.830
A rule that worked well last quarter may need changes after a new system,

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:11.950
new logging pipeline, or new operational pattern appears.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:16.450
Tuning may involve refining conditions, updating exclusions, adjusting thresholds,

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:20.975
improving enrichment, changing severity, adding triage notes, or revising documentation.

00:00:20.975 --> 00:00:23.880
The goal is to improve value without hiding meaningful activity.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.900
Operational feedback is one of the best sources of improvement.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:31.120
Analysts can identify confusing alerts, repeated false positives, missing context,

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:33.420
and signals that require too much manual work.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.575
Detection engineers can use that feedback to make rules more useful.

00:00:36.575 --> 00:00:40.135
Continuous improvement turns detections into living defensive assets.

00:00:40.135 --> 00:00:43.120
Review cycles, ownership, version history, testing notes,

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.725
and performance metrics help teams keep rules aligned with changing threats and changing

00:00:47.725 --> 00:00:47.840
environments.
